They have a general formula $\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{x}}(\mathrm{H} 2 \mathrm{O})_{\mathrm{y}}$. This formula suggests that they are hydrates of carbon. Plants prepare carbohydrtes through photosynthsis, in the presence of chlorophyll and sunlight.

$$
6 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}_{(\mathrm{l})}+6 \mathrm{CO}_{2(\mathrm{~g})} \xrightarrow{\begin{array}{c}
\text { Sunlight } \\
\text { Chlorophyll }
\end{array}} \mathrm{C}_{6} \mathrm{H}_{12} \mathrm{O}_{6(\mathrm{aq})}+6 \mathrm{O}_{2(\mathrm{~g})}
$$

Plants convert glucose into starch and cellulose. Carbohydrates are monomers and polymers of aldehydes and ketones that have numerous hydroxyl groups attached.
